  faq: [
    { q: "How many days do I need?", a: "Four in the city is a good first trip. Add a fifth for Liberty/Ellis or a slow Brooklyn day." },
    { q: "Is New York expensive?", a: "Hotels and dinner can be. Bodegas, slices, parks, and the subway are the release valve. Tipping is part of the bill." },
    { q: "Is it safe at night?", a: "In busy, lit neighborhoods, generally yes with normal theft awareness. Empty subway cars at 2 a.m. and empty parks are a different sentence. Read the street." },
    { q: "Do I need cash?", a: "A little. Cards and tap cover most of the city. Some carts and older counters still prefer cash." },
    { q: "Which area should I stay in?", a: "West Village or LES for a first trip; UWS with kids; Brooklyn Heights if you want to sleep. Not Times Square unless a theater week forces it." },
    { q: "Is public transport easy?", a: "Yes if you can do stairs and tolerate delays. OMNY tap and follow the signs. Weekend construction is real — check the app." },
    { q: "Do I need to book attractions?", a: "Liberty/Ellis, The Met on busy days, MoMA, observatories, and the Tenement Museum: yes. Parks and the Memorial plaza: no." },
    { q: "JFK, LaGuardia, or Newark?", a: "The one your ticket already chose. Newark is a real New York airport. LGA is closer to the east side. JFK has the LIRR." },
    { q: "Is tap water safe?", a: "Yes. Ask for tap. The city's water is not a bit." },
    { q: "Can I manage without speaking Spanish?", a: "Yes. English is the default in tourist-facing rooms. A little Spanish changes some counters for the better." },
  ],
